//! THE SAW FILE A C IMAGE STILL HAS TO HAVE (sawos design 39, the C-leg probe).
//!
//! **IT IS EMPTY ON PURPOSE, AND THE FACT THAT IT MUST EXIST IS FINDING 5.**
//! The image this package builds is C — `crt0.c`, `syscall.c`, `main.c` — and
//! carries no Saw whatsoever. But `emit = "sosimg"` is Blade's target, and
//! Blade's `build_sos_image` opens with `find_source_file()`, which looks for
//! `src/main.saw`, then `main.saw`, then `src/lib.saw` and refuses the build
//! with "No main.saw or lib.saw found" when there is none. A package of pure C
//! is not expressible; a package of pure C plus this file is.
//!
//! **IT COSTS 138 BYTES, AND MEASURING THAT IS THE OTHER HALF OF THE FINDING.**
//! The object `sawc --freestanding` produces from this file has an EMPTY
//! `.text` and defines no symbol anything references — but it also carries
//! design 158's static backtrace table, `.rodata.__saw_bt_table`, and that
//! section is marked RETAIN, so `--gc-sections` keeps it whether or not a
//! `dump_tasks()` could ever read it. In this image it is 138 bytes of the
//! riscv32 segment's 532: a quarter of a program that has no Saw in it.
//!
//! Nothing else survives. The entry is the C crt0 (`ENTRY(_start)`), the one
//! loadable segment is clang's code and `.rodata`, and `_arena_start` and
//! `_arena_end` sit on top of each other because the package's own linker
//! script sets `ARENA_SIZE` to 0 — so the 138 bytes are the whole of what the
//! stub adds, and they are constant per image rather than per Saw declaration.
//!
//! WHAT IT COSTS A HUMAN is this file, per C package. That is small, and it is
//! not nothing: an M7 toolchain that builds toybox will not want a stub Saw
//! file per binary, so design 31 part 4's "crt0 + linker scripts per the sosimg
//! discipline" wants one more line — an emit path that takes OBJECTS rather
//! than a package. The seam is Blade's, so it is a sawlang change, which is why
//! this probe did not make it (design 39's hard constraints).
//!
//! NOTHING BELOW THIS LINE. A declaration here would be a declaration in the
//! image, which is the one thing this package is claiming not to have.
